From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 20:50:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media (ohos): Slide the playback buffer with the reads of
 AVPlayer

The data source of the OpenHarmony AVPlayer backend kept a fixed 8 MB
window of the media resource. Once the window was full, it dropped the
pushed chunks, but still appended a later, smaller one at the offset of
the dropped bytes. A read past the window threw the window away and
restarted the fetch at the read position while AVPlayer waited, and
without range support, every restart fetched the resource from its
start again.

Keep the bytes from 1 MiB before AVPlayer's furthest read, up to 8 MiB,
and evict older ones as new bytes arrive. Once the buffer is full, keep
what fits, ask the media element to stop fetching, and drop the rest.
With range support, restart the fetch at the end of the buffered bytes
once fewer than 2 MiB are left to read, on a thread of its own so that
AVPlayer keeps reading meanwhile. Without range support, a restart
fetches the resource from its start again, so wait until AVPlayer
reaches the end of the buffered bytes.

Accept pushed bytes again once the media element answers the restart,
which happens before it can push any byte of the new fetch, rather than
once the response of the new fetch sets the input size.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: unit tests for the playback buffer, which only build for
OpenHarmony; see Validation.

Validation: the 16 unit tests of playback_buffer.rs pass when the file is built as a standalone host crate, and each of the four bugs they cover makes its test fail when put back (eviction relative to the last read, a read past the end of the resource moving the read position, dropping the part of a chunk that fits, ending a stopped fetch). A HiHope DAYU200 (OpenHarmony 7.0, API 26), headless release builds (`./mach build --ohos --headless-binary`) of ohos-main with patches 68 to 76, with and without this patch, playing a 90 s, 42 MB H.264 video served with Content-Length to its end: no pause longer than 0.2 s either way from a local server with or without range support, or at 1.5 MB/s; at 750 kB/s with 1 s of latency, one pause of 1.6 s without and 1.7 s with this patch. With it, the video takes 4 instead of 7 requests at 1.5 MB/s, 4 instead of 9 when seeking twice, 2 instead of 7 at 750 kB/s, where playback also starts 1 s sooner, and 7 instead of 6 from the local server; without range support, it downloads 212 instead of 240 MB. A chunked 14 MB, 30 s video without Content-Length or range support at 3 MB/s played 16 s of video in 40 s without this patch, and now plays to its end without a pause. `./mach test-wpt --ohos --processes 1` of the series' 60 media tests with SpiderMonkey: the same results as without this patch, with no crash.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written and tested by the agent, reviewed by another agent)
